WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:01.870
Film Courage: I’m just trying to figure
out the timeline?

00:00:01.870 --> 00:00:06.650
So the six months ends and then you don’t
go back [to work] at the Y [YMCA] it sounds

00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:07.650
like?

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:10.870
Christine Conradt: Right…well it wasn’t
like I was suddenly rolling in money and getting

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:12.580
offers either.

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:18.170
I did end up getting at that time a lot of
interest and I was working and writing.

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:22.630
So what I actually ended up doing was I was
hired to rewrite other people’s scripts.

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:28.720
I didn’t sell my own but a production company
hired me to do some rewrites for a couple

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:32.930
of Lifetime movies (they were doing Lifetime
at the time).

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:41.270
And I didn’t know if there was going to
be more because I didn’t know when the next

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:42.820
one was coming.

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:46.640
During that time I decided I needed to maintain
what I was doing during the day.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:49.760
I needed to be able to take these meetings
with production companies.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:53.440
Part of working at the Y prevented me from
taking meetings because of my responsibility

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:54.440
there.

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:58.250
So I basically need a job that had less responsibility.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:03.790
During that time I worked for a temp company
that catered to the entertainment industry

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:08.210
so I could pick which days I wanted to work
and I was also bartending at night.

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:15.130
So that was something that wouldn’t interfere
and I was waiting tables also.

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:19.210
So kind of doing what a lot of people who
come to LA do who want to be in the entertainment

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:21.299
field do.

00:01:21.299 --> 00:01:26.630
Because it was really hard to have a job where
you can make a decent salary because with

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:30.840
that comes the responsibility which precluded
me obviously from being able to go and have

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.749
the time I knew I needed to write when I was
on those projects.

00:01:33.749 --> 00:01:39.999
And eventually I could just quit those jobs
one at a time where I was making a living

00:01:39.999 --> 00:01:40.999
just writing.

00:01:40.999 --> 00:01:42.310
Film Courage: Wow!

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:46.609
So would you say that it was a few years out
or…?

00:01:46.609 --> 00:01:51.840
Christine Conradt: Yeah, I would say it was
about 4 or 5 years maybe before I could get

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:53.829
rid of all the other jobs.

00:01:53.829 --> 00:02:01.560
But during those 4 or 5 years I was rather
consistently working on screenplays and building

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:02.729
my body of work.

00:02:02.729 --> 00:02:06.840
Film Courage: So you could almost sense that
is was going to be around the corner?

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:08.610
Christine Conradt: Right, I just didn’t
know when.

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:10.420
Film Courage: Interesting.

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:13.710
How were you dealing with being tired from
working so many jobs?

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:17.940
Because it’s exhausting just getting ready
for one of those jobs, but then you still

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:20.040
have to keep your mind intact to do rewrites.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:22.950
Christine Conradt: Yeah…I think…first
of all, it helps when you’re in your 20’s.

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:23.950
Film Courage: That’s true.

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:27.940
Christine Conradt: I don’t know if I could
do that in my 40’s.

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:31.960
But in your 20’s you just have a lot more
energy.

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:35.570
But also the writing was what I looked forward
to.

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:38.690
So I would get up and go to the job (because
you have to go to the job) but then I would

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:42.860
come home and the writing was something that
energized me and so I never really looked

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:48.160
at it like I needed to be…that I was just
too tired to write.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:53.331
I don’t really think I’ve ever really
felt that because for me it’s the thing

00:02:53.331 --> 00:02:55.560
that I just want to do all the time.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.920
I’m just so much more comfortable with a
pen in my hand and a piece of paper in case

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:00.770
I have an idea or something.

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:03.480
So for me, it never felt like that.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:06.970
Film Courage: I was just curious, were your
parents super-disciplined?

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:10.210
Because you have this amazing discipline.

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:13.120
Christine Conradt: I think both of them were,
my parents.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:16.700
My dad was a very hard worker and I think
my sister and I both got our work ethic from

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:17.820
him.

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:22.530
He was a door-to-door insurance salesman (back
when they had those).

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:26.230
And he did very well, but he was always working.

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:30.330
And my mom was very much pro-education.

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:35.620
She has a doctoral degree and so I think we
were just this combination of…he never went

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:39.800
to college but he worked very hard and my
mom was all about…you know, she has multiple

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:47.540
degrees and so we kind of were a mix of both,
of seeing both of those people in our lives.

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:52.440
Film Courage: And door-to-door it sounds like
you would have to be so self-generating…like

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.100
you would have to be your own taskmaster?

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:56.830
Christine Conradt: And he very much was that.

00:03:56.830 --> 00:03:57.890
He was very self-motivated.

00:03:57.890 --> 00:04:02.540
Film Courage: Yeah, because you talk about
that first knock on that door…that would

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:03.880
terrify me!

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:06.140
Christine Conradt: That would terrify me,
too.

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:07.730
That’s definitely not me, that’s not my
personality.

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:13.450
But for him, he’s the type of person that
would say he could sell ice to an Eskimo because

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:18.510
he’s just that type of charismatic person
and he loves getting to know people and talking

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:22.200
to them and selling them, he loves selling
things.

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:25.930
So that for him was a great occupation.

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:29.581
But very self-motivated because you do have
to get up every morning and you have to decide

00:04:29.581 --> 00:04:32.260
where you are going to go and who you are
going to try to sell to and he had a lot of

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:33.260
control over that.

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:33.940
Film Courage: Wow, interesting.

